// CSV-ONLY spec: declaration x dialect cases that depend on the CSV materialization of the fixture.
//
// Note that `csv-*.csv-spec` does NOT mean CSV-only -- TSV loads csv-basic and csv-declared-schema too, because
// both formats go through the same delimited reader. This file exists for the narrower set of cases that TSV
// genuinely cannot run: employees_no_mv.csv is column-aligned with space-padded string fields, and the TSV
// generator writes those fields unpadded, so a case that observes padding has nothing to observe on TSV.
//
// Keep the portable declaration x dialect cases in csv-declared-schema.csv-spec, where TSV runs them too. Only
// put a case here when it depends on a property of the CSV file that the other formats' materializations lack.

// DECLARATION x DIALECT. The two are orthogonal knobs and each must survive the other. This fixture's string
// columns are space-padded to a fixed width ("Georgi" occupies 11 bytes), and the harness injects trim_spaces:true
// unless the WITH already sets it, so pinning trim_spaces:false is what makes the padding observable. The padding
// is asserted through LENGTH rather than by writing a padded literal: a csv-spec expected table trims its cells,
// so trailing whitespace cannot be expressed there and a padded value can only be checked via a derived scalar.
// Discriminating on both axes -- lose the dialect setting and every length drops to the trimmed width, lose the
// declaration and there is no `name` column to measure. Note a keyword declaration does NOT imply trimming: it
// takes the field's bytes as the dialect resolved them.
strictDeclaredSchemaDoesNotImplyTrimming
required_capability: dataset_in_from_command
dataset: employees_declared_untrimmed: "{{employees_no_mv}}" WITH {"trim_spaces": false, "mappings": {"dynamic": "false", "properties": {"id": {"type": "long", "path": "emp_no"}, "name": {"type": "keyword", "path": "first_name"}}}}

FROM employees_declared_untrimmed
| EVAL padded_width = LENGTH(name)
| KEEP id, padded_width
| SORT id
| LIMIT 3;

id:long | padded_width:integer
10001   | 11
10002   | 11
10003   | 11
;

// CSV-ONLY because the drift itself is comma-specific: mv_sample's bracketed cell contains COMMAS, so only the
// CSV materialization splits that row into more fields than its header declares. The TSV generator converts the
// delimiter commas to tabs and leaves the ones inside the cell alone, so the same row is a well-formed 3 fields
// there and there is no drift to detect. TSV's own coverage of this bound is the unit-level
// testDeclaredBindingRowWidthValidationOnTsv, which builds a genuinely tab-ragged row.
//
// Row-shape validation survives the declaration. The declared twin of
// csvNoneOnMultiValueDataFailsColumnMismatch in csv-multivalue.csv-spec: mv_sample's first row splits into 5
// fields against a 3-column header once `none` says brackets are not multivalue syntax. Pinning a schema used
// to disable the per-row column-count check outright, so that row was accepted with `tags` reading the
// fragment "[alpha" and no warning at all. It is drift under either binding, and error_mode governs it under
// both. The declared leg names the FILE's header width, not the declaration's -- a declaration may legitimately
// be narrower or wider than the file, so "schema defines" would report a number the guard never compared against.
//
// `score` is projected deliberately: it is the column that receives the misaligned `beta`, so this is the
// projection that used to surface a coercion error naming a cell in no logical column of the file. Projecting
// only the clean columns cannot reach that ordering, so it would pin the happy path instead of the contract.
strictDeclaredSchemaKeepsRowWidthValidation
required_capability: dataset_in_from_command
dataset: mv_sample_declared_none: "{{mv_sample}}" WITH {"multi_value_syntax": "none", "error_mode": "skip_row", "mappings": {"dynamic": "false", "properties": {"id": {"type": "integer", "path": "id"}, "tags": {"type": "keyword", "path": "tags"}, "score": {"type": "integer", "path": "score"}}}}

FROM mv_sample_declared_none
| KEEP id, tags, score
| SORT id
;
warningRegex:CSV read from \[.+\] encountered parse errors handled per policy.*
warningRegex:Row \[\d+\] error: CSV row has \[5\] columns but the file's header defines \[3\] columns.*

id:integer | tags:keyword | score:integer
2          | solo         | 20
;
